{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "152",
    "case_summary": "A 60‐year‐old tailor with no significant medical history presented with a 3‐year history of macerated skin between his toes. He complained of a painful, fissured ulcer at the base of the fourth interdigital space of the right foot, which had been progressively enlarging over the past 18 months. He had received multiple antifungal regimens—including topical sertaconazole for 6 weeks and oral terbinafine 250 mg daily for 6 weeks—without improvement. On examination, there was a verrucous, infected ulceration with infiltrated, hyperkeratotic edges and a whitish, fissured base measuring 3 cm in diameter, extending onto the dorsum of the foot. Dermoscopy revealed unstructured whitish areas and hyperkeratosis. Popliteal and inguinal lymph nodes were not palpable, and the remainder of the examination was unremarkable.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Squamous cell carcinoma\nB. typical warts\nC. Persistent fungal intertrigo\nD. Wart\nE. Keratoacanthoma\nF. Corns, inverse psoriasis, chronic hyperkeratosis\nG. None\nH. None\n",
